  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Isolated to widely scattered storms with gusty outflows in SW\n   and WC MT today, widespread storms along the Canadian border \n   in NW MT.\n\n-  Strong easterly gap winds accelerate in canyon corridors\n   tonight with temporary surface smoke relief.\n\n-  Cooler, active shower/storm pattern continues through Friday \n   before upper ridge slowly rebuilds this weekend.\n\nMonsoonal moisture interacting with a moist Pacific trough \nlifting along the Canadian border will trigger convection this \nafternoon and evening. Across Southwest Montana and areas east of \nUS Highway 93, isolated to widely scattered high-based \nthunderstorms will develop. Dry sub-cloud layers (inverted-V \nthermodynamic profiles) will favor an environment capable of mostly\ndry lightning and microburst outflow exceeding 40 mph. Localized\nheavy rain will be possible most concerning in deep canyons and\nacross burn scars and disturbed / urban ground cover. \n\nIn Northwest Montana, shower and thunderstorm coverage will be\nmore widespread primarily along and near the Canadian border, \nwhere synoptic forcing is strongest. Convection will persist into \ntonight while storm coverage wanes and slowly drifts eastward. \nMeanwhile, a strengthening low-level easterly push will drive a \nstrong northeast wind shift this evening across Northwest Montana,\nincluding Flathead Lake where a Lake Wind Advisory remains in \neffect through midnight. Low-level easterly gap winds will \naccelerate overnight through east-to-west terrain passes, \nparticularly in known-prone locations such as Bad Rock and \nHellgate Canyon. This easterly flow will temporarily clear surface\nsmoke southward, though elevated smoke aloft will likely remain.\n\nScattered to widespread shower and thunderstorm coverage will \nexpand westward across western Montana and north-central Idaho on \nThursday and into Friday. Elevated Precipitable Water (PWAT) \nvalues and persistent synoptic forcing will increase the potential\nfor localized heavy rainfall, particularly with slower-moving \nconvective cores. Temperatures will remain moderated under cloud \ncover and easterly low-level flow. Not every location will measure\nnotable nevermind heavy precipitation but the coverage and \nduration of active weather is favorable for much of Western\nMontana being able to pull down some long-awaited rain.\n\nThe upper-level ridge will gradually rebuild over the Northern\nRockies this weekend into early next week under southwesterly flow\naloft. While afternoon temperatures will warm, monsoonal moisture\ntapped within the southwesterly flow will keep PWAT values\nelevated. This will sustain near-daily afternoon shower and\nthunderstorm chances through the weekend and into next week,\npreventing a return to completely dry conditions. Wildfire smoke\nwill remain a challenge through the long-term with minimal\nsuppression anticipated at this time in the source regions of the\nsmoke (WA and OR).",
